# The rpm analogue of debian/fuckwayland.lintian-overrides: every rpmlint
# finding this package accepts, with the sentence saying why.  Nothing else is
# filtered, and tests/test_rpm_spec.py compares this list against its own copy
# in both directions -- a filter that has stopped being needed is a finding too.
#
# rpmlint 2.x still reads the addFilter() form, which is what Fedora's own
# packages ship; the argument is a regular expression matched against the whole
# "<package>: <level>: <finding> <detail>" line.
#
# Measured with rpmlint 2.7.0 over the three packages built on the Ubuntu 26.04
# guest [recon2/pkg-rpm.md 4]: 30 errors / 16 warnings, of which most are
# DEBIAN's rpmlint configuration talking (no-group-tag, no-packager-tag,
# no-signature, sixteen spelling-errors) and are not how Fedora's own config
# scores a package.  The three below are the real ones and are the ones the CI
# `rpm` job, which runs Fedora's config, is expected to print.
#
# There were four until HEAD 375d815: the LicenseRef the spec carried while the
# tree had no LICENSE drew invalid-license once per package.  BSD-2-Clause is a
# valid SPDX expression, so that filter went with the placeholder rather than
# staying on as a filter nobody needs.
#
# Two findings survive this file on the guest and are NOT filtered, because
# whether Fedora's own configuration scores them at all is not known here:
# requires-on-release (twice, on the two subpackages, over the
# `Requires: %{name} = %{version}-%{release}` Fedora itself recommends) and
# no-documentation (twice, the same two subpackages, which carry an extension
# and no README).  `--lint` is `|| true`, so neither can redden a build; the CI
# rpm job uploads its rpmlint log, and if no-documentation is real under
# Fedora's config the answer is a %doc on those subpackages rather than a
# fifth filter.

# /etc/xdg/autostart/fuckwayland-enable-bridge.desktop is deliberately a plain
# file and deliberately not %config: it is not user configuration, it is the
# one-shot per-user enabler, and rpm taking it away on erase is the behaviour
# debian/ has to fake with a symlink (debhelper makes every regular file under
# /etc a conffile, and a conffile survives `apt remove`).
addFilter(r"non-conffile-in-etc /etc/xdg/autostart/fuckwayland-enable-bridge\.desktop")

# The tools' names and the words of their trade.  Fedora's rpmlint spell-checks
# Summary and %description against en_US and scores every miss as an error
# (twenty of them in the CI rpm job, run 34364127816: wdotool, wxrandr, uinput,
# execve, argv ...).  The list is the exact set that run printed; a new word in
# a description is a new finding, which is how it should be.
addFilter(r"spelling-error \(['\"](wdotool|wdotool's|wwmctl|wxprop|wxrandr|warandr|wmirror|xdotool|wmctrl|xprop|xrandr|arandr|wlroots|wl|uinput|subpackage|mousemove|execve|dnf|dev|argv)['\"]")

# No man page anywhere in this tree, for any of the six commands.  Each one's
# --help is the cloned tool's own usage text byte for byte, which is the
# documentation this project actually promises (docs/WDOTOOL.md and its five
# siblings are the long form).
addFilter(r"no-manual-page-for-binary (warandr|wdotool|wmirror|wwmctl|wxprop|wxrandr)$")
